From: performance agent
Subject: [PATCH] counter/index.html — start the roster and the module graph with the HTML

web/counter/index.html is owned by another agent this week, so the two head changes the
first-load work needs are here instead of in the file. Both were measured through
web/tools/perf.mjs --patch, which applies exactly these hunks to the served bytes:

  hunk 1 (hints)  field 2.65 s -> 1.30 s, first card 9.8 s -> 6.9 s   (fast 4G + 4x CPU, cold)
  hunk 2 (css)    first paint 1.4 s -> 1.1 s

Apply with `git apply docs/qa/perf/index-head.patch`, or by hand — the two hunks are
independent and hunk 2 is the more opinionated of the two (see the note under it).

--- a/web/counter/index.html
+++ b/web/counter/index.html
@@ -26,6 +26,20 @@
   </noscript>
+  <!-- The roster is what the search field is waiting for, and it used to be requested two
+       round trips after this file: html -> app.js -> ttm.js -> fetch. Preloading it starts the
+       download with the HTML. No crossorigin and no type: ttm.js fetches it with no Accept
+       header of its own, so the preload and the fetch are the same request and it is
+       downloaded once. The photo index next to it is deliberately NOT preloaded — nothing on
+       the first screen needs it. -->
+  <link rel="preload" href="../store/ttm-catalog.json" as="fetch">
+  <!-- The module graph behind the search field, flattened from four serial round trips to one.
+       The other four screens are not here on purpose: app.js loads them once Identify is up. -->
+  <link rel="modulepreload" href="js/bus.js">
+  <link rel="modulepreload" href="js/ttm.js">
+  <link rel="modulepreload" href="js/search.js">
+  <link rel="modulepreload" href="js/index-data.js">
+  <link rel="modulepreload" href="js/vision.js">
+  <link rel="modulepreload" href="js/screens/identify.js">
+  <!-- pdf.js (the reader) and three.js (the 3D viewer) are imported from these two at the
+       moment a manual or a Pick screen opens. The TLS handshake can happen now instead. -->
+  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net" crossorigin>
+  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com" crossorigin>
   <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/counter.css">
   <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/themes.css">
   <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/particons.css">
-  <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/viewer3d.css">
+  <link rel="stylesheet" media="print" onload="this.media='all'" href="css/viewer3d.css">
   <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/screens/identify.css">
-  <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/screens/confirm.css">
-  <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/screens/pick.css">
-  <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/screens/book.css">
-  <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/screens/invoice.css">
-  <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/screens/cost.css">
-  <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/climate.css">
+  <!-- Six sheets for five screens that cannot be reached from the landing, 60 KB of them, all
+       of it render-blocking in front of a search field that uses none of it. media="print"
+       makes the browser fetch them at a lower priority and apply them on load, which is one
+       frame after the first paint and several seconds before anything they style is on screen.
+       Identify's own sheet, counter.css, themes.css and particons.css stay blocking. -->
+  <link rel="stylesheet" media="print" onload="this.media='all'" href="css/screens/confirm.css">
+  <link rel="stylesheet" media="print" onload="this.media='all'" href="css/screens/pick.css">
+  <link rel="stylesheet" media="print" onload="this.media='all'" href="css/screens/book.css">
+  <link rel="stylesheet" media="print" onload="this.media='all'" href="css/screens/invoice.css">
+  <link rel="stylesheet" media="print" onload="this.media='all'" href="css/screens/cost.css">
+  <link rel="stylesheet" media="print" onload="this.media='all'" href="css/climate.css">
   <!-- The saved theme, before the first paint. js/theme.js is a module and therefore deferred,

Note on hunk 2. A `media="print"` sheet is applied on its load event, so a screen can in
principle paint one frame before its own sheet is in. None of these five screens can be
reached in under two seconds — the roster has to arrive and a bike has to be chosen first —
and every one of the sheets is on the wire from the first HTML parse, so the window does not
exist in practice. If it is not worth the argument, take hunk 1 alone: it is where most of
the number is.
